  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>The marbled headstander, Abramites hypselonotus, is an anostomid from the Amazon, Orinoco and Paraguay-Paraná basins in South America. An ecological generalist, it occurs in turbid main river channels, minor tributaries and backwaters, and enters inundated zones during the annual floods. Best kept as a group in a large tank alongside cichlids, characins and catfishes, it may nip long or trailing fins and eat soft-leaved plants.</p><ul><li>Max size: 140 mm SL (6 in SL)</li><li>Aquarium size: 120 × 30 cm (48 × 12 in)</li><li>Temperature: 20–30°C (68–86°F)</li><li>pH: 5.5–7.5</li><li>Hardness: 1–15 dGH</li><li>Difficulty: 2 of 8 (easy)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>Described from 'Xeberos', Peru, which presumably refers to modern-day Jeberos district, Alto Amazonas Province, Loreto region in northeastern (Amazonian) Peru.</p><p>As currently recognised though it has an enormous natural range, occurring throughout much of the Amazon and Orinoco river basins in Bolivia, Brazil, Colombia, Ecuador, Guyana, Peru and Venezuela plus the Paraguay-Paraná system in southern Brazil, Paraguay and Argentina.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>This species is an ecological generalist and found in many habitat types from main river channels with turbid, flowing water to minor tributaries and backwaters.</p><p>It also enters temporarily-inundated zones during the annual floods.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>An aquarium with base dimensions of 120 × 30 cm (48 × 12 in) or equivalent should be the smallest considered, even for a single specimen.</p><p>Volume: ~108 litres (~29 US gal).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>Not critical provided basic maintenance requirements are fulfilled although soft-leaved plants may be consumed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 20–30°C (68–86°F)</li><li>pH: 5.5–7.5</li><li>Hardness: 1–15 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>Anostomids are opportunistic omnivores feeding chiefly on benthic items including sponges, insect larvae, organic detritus, seeds, leaves and filamentous algae in nature.</p><p>Captive specimens are generally adaptable and will accept most good quality prepared foods as well as live or frozen bloodworm, Daphnia, Artemia, etc. but should be offered a varied diet ideally including some greenery in the form of fresh algae, fruit, vegetables or a dried product containing a high proportion of Spirulina or similar.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>Best maintained as a group in a large tank alongside robust cichlids, characins and catfishes.</p><p>It may nip at long or trailing fins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>Sexually mature females are normally deeper-bodied than males.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>No recorded accounts of captive breeding exist.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<p>Abramites spp. are separated from other anostomids primarily on the basis of their relatively deeper bodies and presence of a prominent, post-pelvic median keel, a feature unique to the genus.</p><p>At time of writing A. eques is the only other species recognised and is native to the Río Magdalena drainage in western Colombia.</p><p>A. eques can be distinguished by possession of 13-14 branched anal-fin rays (vs. 10-12 in A. hypselonotus) and 5 transverse bars, with the anteriormost positioned under the dorsal-fin, on the body (vs. 8 bars distributed over the entire body).</p><p>Following Sidlauskas and Vari (2008)[3] the family Anostomidae contains 14 genera, all native to South America.</p><p>Sidlauskas and Vari's phylogenetic analysis suggests that within this grouping Abramites forms a very distinct clade most closely-related to Leporinus.</p><p>Anostomids are distinguished from other Characiformes by the unique presence of only three or four teeth arranged in a step-like fashion in each premaxillary or dentary.</p><p>Many species are known for their inclined feeding posture, a behaviour which has given rise them being referred to by the generalised term 'headstander'.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/abramites-hypselonotus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Günther, A. (1868). Diagnoses of some new freshwater fishes from Surinam and Brazil, in the collection of the British Museum. Annals and Magazine of Natural History (Series 4) v. 1(6), 475-481</li><li>Reis, R. E., S. O. Kullander and C. J. Ferraris, Jr. (eds) (2003). Check list of the freshwater fishes of South and Central America. CLOFFSCA. EDIPUCRS, Porto Alegre, i-xi + 1-729</li><li>Sidlauskas, B. L. and R. P. Vari (2008). Phylogenetic relationships within the South American fish family Anostomidae (Teleostei, Ostariophysi, Characiformes). Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society 154, 70–210</li><li>Vari, R. P. and A. M. Williams (1987). Headstanders of the neotropical anostomid genus Abramites (Pisces: Characiformes: Anostomidae). Proceedings of the Biological Society of Washington 100(1), 89-103</li></ul>"
    }
  ],
